\n\nQ1: Free shipping from €60.00 (FREE_SHIPPING_FROM), configurable via env var PARCELS_FREE_FROM. Note: it's read at import time from environment; `shipping_cost` returns 0 if order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM. Also note the comment says \"after discounts\" - the order value passed is already after discounts.\n\nQ2: Heavy order → split into multiple parcels via `parcel_weights`, each ≤ MAX_PARCEL_KG (31.5 kg), all of the same weight rounded up to the gram. Shipping cost = sum of per-parcel prices. Note a subtlety: due to rounding up to the gram, total billed weight can exceed the actual weight. Also note the \"MAX_PARCEL_KG\" limit is per parcel, not per order — no maximum order weight limit.\n\nQ3: Default carrier = \"postnl\" (settings.py:10), configurable via PARCELS_CARRIER env var, must be one of dhl/ups/postnl, else RuntimeError at import.\n\nQ4: Friday at 17:00 → after cutoff (15:00), so start = Saturday. add_working_days skips Sat/Sun. HANDLING_DAYS=1 + transit. For NL transit=1 → 2 working days from Saturday → Tuesday. For DE/BE/LU transit=2 → 3 working days → Wednesday. For other countries default 4 → 5 working days → Friday. Holidays are NOT accounted for (docstring says \"holidays are not\").\n\nLet me double-check the exact handling of the Friday 17:00 case: start = ordered.date() = Friday; hour 17 >= 15 → start += 1 day = Saturday. Then add_working_days(Saturday, HANDLING_DAYS+transit): first iteration current += 1 day = Sunday (weekday 6, skip, days stays), then Monday (weekday 0 <5, days -=1)... So for NL: days=2 → Monday (1 left), Tuesday (0) → Tuesday. Yes.\n\nNote the interesting subtlety: the cutoff pushes start to Saturday, but since add_working_days only counts weekdays starting from the next day, the handling effectively begins Monday regardless. Actually, for a Friday order before 15:00, start = Friday → Saturday(skip), Sunday(skip), Monday(1), ... same as Friday 17:00! Let me check: Friday at 10:00 → start Friday, days=2 for NL → Sat(skip), Sun(skip), Mon(1), Tue(0) → Tuesday. Same as 17:00. Hmm interesting, so cutoff has no effect for a Friday order with NL transit... Actually for other countries also same. So cutoff only matters on weekdays where it can shift. Let me verify: Friday 14:00 vs Friday 17:00 → both Tuesday for NL. Yes since the shifted day is a weekend day anyway.\n\nHmm, but wait — there's a subtlety: the cutoff for Friday shifts to Saturday, and add_working_days starts from start, adding 1 day first. So effectively the count starts the next Monday. For a Friday order before cutoff, start = Friday, first step = Saturday (skipped). Same result. So for Friday both give the same answer.\n\nAlso worth noting: a holiday on the transit day would NOT be skipped — the code has no holiday calendar. The docstring explicitly says \"holidays are not\" skipped (delivery.py:15).\n\nAlso note the test at tests/test_parcels.py:21: delivery_date(datetime(2026, 3, 5, 16, 0), \"NL\") == date(2026, 3, 10) — 2026-03-05 is a Thursday, at 16:00 → after cutoff → start Friday 2026-03-06 → NL transit 1 + handling 1 = 2 working days → Mar 9 (Mon) and Mar 10 (Tue) → Tuesday. Good.\n\nLet me also double-check the free shipping threshold: is it \"at or above\"? `order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM` → yes, 60.00 exactly is free. And env var name PARCELS_FREE_FROM. Also note FREE_SHIPPING_FROM is not validated (no error if malformed — would raise InvalidOperation at import time when constructing Decimal). Let me check: Decimal(os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_FREE_FROM\", \"60.00\")) — an invalid value raises decimal.InvalidOperation at import. Worth a brief mention.\n\nAlso note: free shipping applies \"inside the EU\" — but actually the CannotShip check happens first for non-EU countries, so the free threshold only applies to EU destinations in practice (non-EU raises before the check). Actually the order: country check first → CannotShip for non-EU. So the free shipping threshold only ever applies to EU countries. Comment says \"ship for free inside the EU\".\n\nQ2 details: also mention that the per-parcel surcharge: parcel_price uses country != \"NL\" (case-sensitivity: shipping_cost upper-cases country first so it's fine; but parcel_price called directly with lowercase 'de' would add the surcharge even for NL... not needed).\n\nLet me also verify: what happens if order weight is heavier than one parcel may be? → split into N parcels of equal weight each ≤ 31.5 kg, rounded up to the gram. Shipping cost = sum of parcel prices, so shipping gets more expensive. Note: rounding up to the gram means the total billed weight may exceed actual total (e.g., 70 kg → 3 × 23.334 = 70.002 kg).\n\nAlso note that per_kg price is per started kilogram (math.ceil(weight_kg)) per parcel — so splitting 32 kg into 2 × 16 kg = base×2 + 0.45×16×2 vs 1 parcel of 32 kg. Let's check: postnl 32kg as 2 parcels of 16: each = 4.10 + 0.45*16 = 11.30; ×2 = 22.60 NL. Single 32 kg parcel would be 4.10 + 0.45*32 = 18.50 — but that's not allowed since max is 31.5.\n\nLet me also check whether there's any \"too heavy\" error path — no. MAX_PARCEL_KG only bounds parcel size, not order size.\n\nNow, is there an issue where a single parcel might exceed max after rounding? total=31.5 → count=1, each=31.500 ≤ 31.5. total = 31.5001 → count = 2 → 15.7501 each → rounded 15.751, fine. But e.g. total = 63.0 → count=2, each=31.5. Fine. total = 62.9 → count=2 → 31.45. Fine. Edge: total = 0.5 → count=1 → 0.5. What about total where each rounds up over MAX? count = ceil(total/31.5) ≥ 2 means total > 31.5, each = total/count ≤ total/2... could each be 31.5+? If total = 63.001 → count = 3 → 21. Fine. If total = 32 → count 2 → 16. So each ≤ 31.5 always? each = total/count, count = ceil(total/31.5) → total/count ≤ 31.5. Yes, plus rounding to gram could push it to 31.501 if total/count is exactly 31.5 → 31.500 fine. But if total = 62.9999 → count = 2 → 31.49995 → rounded up 31.500 → ok. If total = 31.5 → count 1 → 31.500. Hmm what about total = 31.5 - epsilon... fine. Actually rounding up could exceed 31.5 in the count=1 case: total=31.5 exactly is the max for count 1 (ceil(total/31.5)=1 requires total ≤31.5). So each = total ≤ 31.5, rounding up to gram: total=31.4999 → 31.500 ok. So max is fine. Not needed for the answer but nice.\n\nAlso: price per parcel uses math.ceil(weight_kg) — started kg.\n\nLet me also double check that `shipping_cost` calls parcel_weights only when below threshold; for a free order over 60, weights aren't computed (so an invalid weight <= 0 wouldn't raise). Minor.\n\nNow let me verify env var overrides work by running with env vars.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e60c519e001VUcePSupewK41I","sessionID":"ses_e19f4fb81ffeOrjVO5vcAH6pd8","messageID":"msg_1e60c1464001RaWmqHnWUXwak3"}}
