{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794860969326,"sessionID":"ses_e19f4fb81ffeOrjVO5vcAH6pd8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_387bd78ab74d4645b5f4c39d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Answers about the parcels code\n\n## Q1. From what order value is shipping free, and how can that value be changed without editing code?\n\nShipping is free from **€60.00** of order value. The threshold lives in `FREE_SHIPPING_FROM`,\nwhich defaults to `Decimal(\"60.00\")` (`parcels/settings.py:15`), and the quote compares the\norder value against it with `order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM` (`parcels/quote.py:31-32`),\nreturning `Decimal(\"0.00\")` when the order is at or above the threshold\n(`parcels/quote.py:31-32`). The value is the order value *after discounts* and only for\ndestinations inside the EU (`parcels/settings.py:14`, `parcels/quote.py:29-30`): a country\noutside the EU raises `CannotShip` before the free-shipping check is reached\n(`parcels/quote.py:29-30`).\n\n**Changing it without editing code:** set the environment variable `PARCELS_FREE_FROM`, e.g.\n`PARCELS_FREE_FROM=75.00`. It is read straight from the environment in\n`parcels/settings.py:15` and used by `shipping_cost` via the import in `parcels/quote.py:10`.\nTwo caveats worth knowing before you rely on it:\n\n- Settings are read **once, at import time** (`parcels/settings.py:1`), so the environment\n  variable must be set *before* the process starts; changing it later in a running process has\n  no effect.\n- The value is parsed with `Decimal(...)`, so it must be a valid decimal string; a malformed\n  value raises `decimal.InvalidOperation` while `parcels.settings` is imported\n  (`parcels/settings.py:15`).\n\n## Q2. What happens to an order that is heavier than a single parcel may be?\n\nIt is **split into several parcels**, not rejected. `parcel_weights` computes\n`count = max(1, ceil(total_kg / MAX_PARCEL_KG))` and gives every parcel the same weight\n(`parcels/packing.py:20-22`), where `MAX_PARCEL_KG` is 31.5 kg (`parcels/settings.py:17`).\nThe per-parcel weight is rounded **up** to the gram with `ROUND_CEILING`\n(`parcels/packing.py:21`), so the billed total can be a hair above the real weight: 70 kg\nbecomes three parcels of 23.334 kg, i.e. 70.002 kg billed (`parcels/packing.py:12-16`;\nsee also the test at `tests/test_parcels.py:11-12`).\n\nEach parcel is then priced on its own and the prices are summed\n(`parcels/quote.py:33`), so splitting makes shipping proportionally more expensive: one\nparcel costs `base + per_kg * ceil(weight_kg)` (`parcels/quote.py:19`), i.e. the per-kilogram\nprice is charged per **started** kilogram per parcel, and the EU surcharge for a destination\noutside the Netherlands is added **per parcel** (`parcels/quote.py:20-21`). Example with the\ndefault carrier `postnl` (`parcels/carriers.py:19`): 70 kg to NL = 3 × (4.10 + 0.45 × 24) =\n€44.70, versus €5.00 for a 2 kg order (`parcels/quote.py:17-22`, `parcels/quote.py:33`).\n\nThere is no upper limit on the total order weight; only the per-parcel limit of 31.5 kg\napplies (`parcels/settings.py:17`). A non-positive weight is the one hard error: it raises\n`ValueError(\"an order weighs something\")` (`parcels/packing.py:18-19`).\n\n## Q3. Which carrier is used when nothing is configured, and how is another one chosen?\n\nThe default is **`postnl`**: `CARRIER = os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_CARRIER\", \"postnl\").lower()`\n(`parcels/settings.py:10`). Another carrier is chosen by setting the environment variable\n`PARCELS_CARRIER` to one of the supported names `dhl`, `ups`, `postnl`\n(`parcels/settings.py:8`), e.g. `PARCELS_CARRIER=dhl`. The value is lower-cased, so\n`PARCELS_CARRIER=DHL` works (`parcels/settings.py:10`).\n\nIf the variable holds anything else, the module raises\n`RuntimeError: PARCELS_CARRIER must be one of dhl, ups, postnl, not ...` while being imported\n(`parcels/settings.py:11-12`) — so a typo crashes the program at start-up rather than\nsilently falling back to the default. The chosen carrier is looked up in the `TARIFFS` table\nwhen a price is calculated (`parcels/quote.py:18`), whose entries are\n`dhl` (4.95 / 0.40 / 6.00), `ups` (6.50 / 0.30 / 5.00) and `postnl` (4.10 / 0.45 / 7.25)\nfor base price, price per started kg and the EU surcharge (`parcels/carriers.py:16-20`).\n\nAgain, the value is read once at import time (`parcels/settings.py:1`), so it must be set\nbefore the process starts.\n\n## Q4. How is the delivery date worked out for an order placed on a Friday at 17:00? Does it account for weekends and public holidays?\n\nThe calculation is: cut-off → start day → skip weekends → add handling days + transit days.\n\n1. **Cut-off.** Orders placed at or after 15:00 are handled from the next day\n   (`CUTOFF_HOUR = 15`, `parcels/delivery.py:11`; applied in `parcels/delivery.py:27-28`).\n   17:00 is past the cut-off, so the start day becomes **Saturday**.\n2. **Working days only.** `add_working_days` advances one day at a time and only counts days\n   whose weekday is < 5, so Saturdays and Sundays are skipped\n   (`parcels/delivery.py:14-22`). Starting from Saturday, the weekend is therefore jumped\n   entirely and counting effectively begins on Monday.\n3. **Handling + transit.** `HANDLING_DAYS` is 1 (`parcels/settings.py:18`) and the transit\n   time depends on the destination: NL 1 day, BE/DE/LU 2 days, everything else the default of\n   4 days (`parcels/delivery.py:9-10`). The total is `HANDLING_DAYS + transit`\n   (`parcels/delivery.py:30`).\n\nSo a Friday 17:00 order is delivered:\n\n| Destination | Transit days | Delivery date | Weekday |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| NL | 1 | 2 working days later | Tuesday |\n| BE, DE, LU | 2 | 3 working days later | Wednesday |\n| any other EU country | 4 (default) | 5 working days later | Friday |\n\nConcretely, for Friday 2026-03-06 17:00: NL → 2026-03-10 (Tuesday), DE → 2026-03-11\n(Wednesday), FR → 2026-03-13 (Friday).\n\n**Weekends: yes.** Saturday and Sunday are excluded from the count (`parcels/delivery.py:14-22`).\n\n**Public holidays: no.** There is no holiday calendar anywhere in the package, and the\ndocstring says so explicitly: \"Saturdays and Sundays are skipped (holidays are not)\"\n(`parcels/delivery.py:15`). A delivery date that lands on a public holiday is therefore\nreturned as-is, which can promise a delivery on a day the carrier does not deliver.\n\nOne more detail: the cut-off only compares the **hour**, not minutes, so an order placed at\n15:00 is already treated as \"after cut-off\" (`parcels/delivery.py:27`). For a Friday order\nthis happens to make no difference, because the next day (Saturday) is skipped by the\nweekend rule anyway — a Friday 09:00 order gets the same dates as a Friday 17:00 order.\n","filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/answer-questions/opencode-1/repo/ANSWERS.md"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/answer-questions/opencode-1/repo/ANSWERS.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"ANSWERS.md","time":{"start":1794860969048,"end":1794860969289}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting — for a Friday, the cutoff doesn't matter because the next day is Saturday, which is skipped anyway. Let me verify: Friday 17:00 → start = Saturday (since 17 >= 15). Then add_working_days(Saturday, HANDLING_DAYS + transit). For NL: 1+1 = 2 working days from Saturday: Saturday → +1 = Sunday (skipped), +1 = Monday (count 1), +1 = Tuesday (count 2) → Tuesday March 10. Correct.\n\nWait, add_working_days starts at `start` and increments first: current = start; then current += 1 day each loop. So from Saturday with days=2: Sunday (weekday 6, skipped, days still 2), Monday (counted, days=1), Tuesday (counted, days=0) → Tuesday. Yes.\n\nFor DE: transit=2, +1 handling = 3 working days: Monday(2), Tuesday(1), Wednesday(0) → Wednesday March 11.\n\nNote: the test in tests says delivery_date(datetime(2026, 3, 5, 16, 0), \"NL\") == date(2026, 3, 10) — March 5 2026 is a Thursday. 16:00 >= cutoff so start = Friday March 6. add_working_days(Friday, 2) → Saturday skip, Sunday skip, Monday (1), Tuesday (2) → March 10. Yes.\n\nHolidays: not accounted for — the docstring at delivery.py:15 explicitly says \"holidays are not\" skipped.\n\nNote also that the free shipping check: FREE_SHIPPING_FROM default 60.00, change via env var PARCELS_FREE_FROM, read at import time (settings.py:15, settings.py:1). Note: the setting is read from environment at import time — so changing requires restarting the process. Also note value is read as a string and converted to Decimal — an invalid value raises InvalidOperation at import.\n\nAlso note an important subtlety: the free shipping applies only inside EU — actually shipping_cost raises CannotShip for non-EU before checking free shipping. So free shipping threshold applies to EU destinations only (the check order: country check first).\n\nQ2: Heavy order split into multiple parcels of equal weight, each ≤ MAX_PARCEL_KG (31.5 kg), rounded up to the gram — so total billed weight may exceed actual weight slightly (70 kg → 70.002 kg). Each parcel is priced separately, so the shipping cost is the sum of per-parcel prices (base + per_kg * ceil(kg) + EU surcharge per parcel).\n\nLet me compute for 70kg NL: 3 parcels × (4.10 + 0.45*ceil(23.334)=24 → 4.10 + 10.80 = 14.90) = 44.70. Yes.\n\nAlso note: per-parcel kg is charged as \"started kilogram\" (math.ceil(weight_kg)) — quote.py:19.\n\nAlso note the MAX_PARCEL_KG is not configurable via env — only via code (settings.py:17). The question Q2 asks \"What happens to an order that is heavier than a single parcel may be?\" — it's split, not rejected. There's no upper limit on total order weight; parcels count grows.\n\nQ3: Default carrier is \"postnl\" (settings.py:10), configurable via env var PARCELS_CARRIER to one of \"dhl\", \"ups\", \"postnl\" (settings.py:8, 11-12), invalid raises RuntimeError at import. Tariffs in carriers.py:16-20. Note the carrier is baked at import time — so it must be set before the process starts.\n\nQ4: Friday 17:00 → beyond CUTOFF_HOUR 15 (delivery.py:11, 27-28), so start moves to Saturday; working days skip weekends (delivery.py:14-22). NL → Tuesday, DE/BE/LU → Wednesday, default countries (4 transit days) → Friday. Weekends are accounted for; public holidays are NOT (delivery.py:15).\n\nLet me double-check the default-country Friday: FR transit default 4 + handling 1 = 5 working days from Saturday → Mon(4), Tue(3), Wed(2), Thu(1), Fri(0) → Friday March 13. Correct.\n\nAlso note the assumption that CUTOFF_HOUR check uses only the hour, not minutes — 15:00 is included.\n\nAlso mention: HANDLING_DAYS = 1 (settings.py:18) — one handling working day on top of transit.\n\nOne more check: does delivery_date consider transit from the destination country's list — TRANSIT_DAYS = NL 1, BE/DE/LU 2, default 4 (delivery.py:9-10).\n\nNow let me also verify the exact citation line numbers. Let me double check line numbers of key statements:\n\nsettings.py:\n- 8: CARRIERS = (\"dhl\", \"ups\", \"postnl\")\n- 10: CARRIER = os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_CARRIER\", \"postnl\").lower()\n- 11-12: validation\n- 15: FREE_SHIPPING_FROM\n- 17: MAX_PARCEL_KG = Decimal(\"31.5\")\n- 18: HANDLING_DAYS = 1\n- 1: docstring \"Settings read from the environment once, at import.\"\n\nquote.py:\n- 25-33 shipping_cost\n- 29-30 country check\n- 31-32 free shipping\n- 17-22 parcel_price\n- 19: ceil(weight_kg)\n- 20-21 eu surcharge\n\npacking.py:\n- 11-22 parcel_weights\n- 20: count\n- 21: quantize ROUND_CEILING\n\ndelivery.py:\n- 9: TRANSIT_DAYS\n- 10: DEFAULT_TRANSIT_DAYS\n- 11: CUTOFF_HOUR\n- 14-22: add_working_days\n- 15: docstring re holidays\n- 25-30: delivery_date\n- 27-28: cutoff\n\nNow write ANSWERS.md at top of project (repo root).\n\nAlso verify: \"From what order value is shipping free, and how can that value be changed without editing code?\" — €60.00 default; env var PARCELS_FREE_FROM. Also note that the threshold applies to order value after discounts (settings.py:14 comment), and only for EU destinations (non-EU raises CannotShip before the check, quote.py:29-30).\n\nNote the caveat that env var is read at import time — requires restart, and value must be a valid decimal string or ImportError at import (Decimal raises InvalidOperation at import).\n\nLet me write the file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e60cb896001fCGprahpMy3uwP","sessionID":"ses_e19f4fb81ffeOrjVO5vcAH6pd8","messageID":"msg_1e60c8cda00128cKnmQTwUvEds"}}
